4. BUYER AND SELLER REGISTRATION
The registration system must allow a person to register as either a buyer or a seller.
The registration interface must clearly distinguish the two account types so users understand which type of account they are creating.
4.1 Buyer registration
A buyer must be able to:
Select the buyer account type.
Register using the required account information.
Verify their email address.
Log in securely.
Access their buyer dashboard.
Browse and purchase products.
Manage their orders.
Submit eligible product reviews and feedback.
Access the features permitted to buyers.
4.2 Seller registration
A seller must be able to:
Select the seller account type.
Submit the required personal and business information.
Provide the information required for seller verification.
Verify their email address.
Submit their seller application for admin approval.
View their application status.
Access the seller dashboard according to their approval status.
4.3 Seller approval before full access
Seller registration must not automatically grant full seller privileges.
After submitting an application, the seller must remain in the appropriate pending or restricted state until an authorised administrator reviews and approves the application.
The admin must be able to approve or reject the application and request additional information where necessary.
Only approved sellers should receive full access to the seller functions required to operate on the marketplace, including publishing products.
Pending sellers may access the relevant account and application-status information, but must not be able to bypass the approval process.
Buyer and seller permissions must be separated correctly. A seller must not gain administrative privileges by selecting the seller account type.
5. LOGIN, EMAIL VERIFICATION AND ACCOUNT SECURITY
The authentication system must support both buyer and seller accounts.
It must include:
Registration and login.
Logout.
Secure password storage.
Appropriate form validation.
Duplicate account prevention.
Password show/hide functionality.
Password strength guidance.
Secure session handling.
Forgot-password functionality.
Email verification.
Appropriate protection against unauthorised login attempts.
5.1 Email verification during registration
When a user registers, the system must send a genuine verification email to the supplied email address through Brevo.
The account must not be treated as email-verified until the user successfully completes the verification process.
5.2 Email verification during login
The login process must also support the requested email-based authentication verification.
Where email verification is required during login, the system must send a secure, time-limited verification code or link to the account's registered email address and validate it before granting access.
The developer must implement this as a genuine backend authentication process, not as a decorative or non-functional verification screen.
The verification process must protect against expired codes, repeated use, excessive attempts and unauthorised access.
5.3 Password reset
Users must be able to request password-reset emails.
The system must use secure, single-use, time-limited reset tokens.
Existing passwords must never be emailed to users.
